Imagine you’re a new security guard hired to protect a large apartment complex. Before you can secure individual apartments, you first need to know — which units are actually occupied? A vacant floor needs no patrolling. Nmap’s host discovery does exactly this: before scanning ports, it figures out which machines on a network are alive and worth investigating — whether you’re the guard, or someone trying to get past one.

Task — 2 Subnetworks

What is a network? A network is a group of computer linked together using a shared medium. Network works with an IP address, but there are a limited number of IP’s available, so to fix this issue, what we did was, divide an IP within a network giving a separate IP under the same network, this concept is known as subnetting. Think of it like a cake at a family gathering instead of buying a separate pastry for each of the 8 members, you get one cake and slice it into 8 pieces. One IP block, divided among many devices.

When Nmap scans a network, it’s looking across all those “slices” to find which devices are actually online, that’s host discovery.

Subnets with /16, which means that the subnet mask can be written as 255.255.0.0. This subnet can accommodate around 65,534 hosts.
Subnets with /24, which indicates that the subnet mask can be expressed as 255.255.255.0. This subnet can have around 254 hosts.
Now, instead of just having an IP address, devices have a physical address or a MAC address. So, when we are on a subnet, the scanner uses ARP (address resolution protocol), which maps the IPv4 address to the MAC address. ARP asks the “hey, who’s IP is this?” and gets a MAC address as a result and actually know that if there is a packet for this IP, I have to transfer to this MAC, and actually creates a mapping table for it.

It’s like having a nickname for your home, suppose your name for the world is “Hugh E. Rection”, your friends call you Hugh E. Rection, your girlfriend (Ok, sorry, forgot that you are single) calls you Hugh E. Rection but, in your home, everyone calls you “Bruce”, in this case when you network with someone outside your home network, you are called Hugh E. Rection which relates to IP address, but in your home, where you use MAC address, everyone calls you Bruce.

If you are in Network A, you can use ARP only to discover the devices within that subnet (10.1.100.0/24). Suppose you are connected to a subnet different from the subnet of the target system(s). In that case, all packets generated by your scanner will be routed via the default gateway (router) to reach systems on another subnet; however, ARP queries won’t be routed and therefore cannot cross the subnet router. ARP is a link-layer protocol, and ARP packets are bound to their subnet.

