Suppose you went looking for residents inside each apartment, and some of them have closed doors and windows, they also have curtains so that no one can see inside. There are people still inside but you wont really know if there are residents inside or not, so you find another way, you ring the doorbell, if someone answers, there is at least one resident otherwise there is no one (considering the case the resident is not using the restroom when you rang the door bell).

Just like this, computers have firewalls that check each packet and either let them get forwarded or drop the packet. Firewalls are of two types, stateless and stateful, if it’s statefull, it will track the entire communication link, if not, you are in luck, because it will just look at an individual packet rather than connecting it with an existing link. MS windows now block the ICMP echo requests which are used for the ping tool. Even if you are on the same subnet, the packet might be dropped since ICMP echo preceds ARP query.

